#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186363048 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "b1639105-dca4-50d1-8d86-a7aed03ba0b5" :knowledge-point/title "Estimating the Value of a Three-Variable Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5e056001-1d92-5766-8006-b42dc59e54d6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1.03" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186363050} {:answer/id #uuid "500899d9-3be0-5e5a-911c-4af664d0adcf" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.88" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186363051} {:answer/id #uuid "5ad6384f-5c57-5f5b-8dad-fc88c4721a3b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.98" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186363052} {:answer/id #uuid "a407e943-1fcb-56c2-8166-77dc98c90d48" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1.09" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186363053} {:answer/id #uuid "0082ecdf-47e5-53a5-895e-7a4e03248e03" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.93" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186363054}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "500899d9-3be0-5e5a-911c-4af664d0adcf" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.88" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186363051} :answer-field/id #uuid "bfb73485-902b-5d18-b9b9-048f28380e6a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186363049}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "e2824792-4fee-5f3f-be81-2e4b2980e7af" :question/math-academy-id "q-129654" :question/problem "Suppose that $f(x,y,z)$ is a differentiable function such that\n\n$$\nf(1,0,-1)=1,\\,{f}_{x}(1,0,-1)=-1,\\,{f}_{y}(1,0,-1)=-1,\\,{f}_{z}(1,0,-1)=0.\n$$\n\nUsing a linear approximation of $f$ at the point $(1,0,-1)$ estimate the value of $f(1.02,0.1,-0.96)$" :question/worked-solution "The linear approximation of the function $f$ at the point $(a,b,c)$ is given by\n\n$$\nf(x,y,z)≈f(a,b,c)+∇f(a,b,c)\\cdot ⟨x-a,y-b,z-c⟩.\n$$\n\nSo, the linear approximation of $f$ at the point $(1,0,-1)$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,y,z) & ≈f(1,0,-1)+∇f(1,0,-1)\\cdot ⟨x-1,y-0,z-(-1)⟩ \\\\  & =f(1,0,-1)+⟨{f}_{x}(1,0,-1),{f}_{y}(1,0,-1),{f}_{z}(1,0,-1)⟩\\cdot ⟨x-1,y,z+1⟩ \\\\  & =1+⟨-1,-1,0⟩\\cdot ⟨x-1,y,z+1⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can approximate $f(1.02,0.1,-0.96)$ by evaluating the linear approximation at $x=1.02,$ $y=0.1,$ and $z=-0.96$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(1.02,0.1,-0.96) & ≈1+⟨-1,-1,0⟩\\cdot ⟨1.02-1,0.1,-0.96+1⟩ \\\\  & =1+⟨-1,-1,0⟩\\cdot ⟨0.02,0.1,0.04⟩ \\\\  & =1-1(0.02)-1(0.1)+0(0.04) \\\\  & =1-0.02-0.1+0 \\\\  & =0.88\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186363055 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "0015e19e-222b-5c5b-a020-93392c50c10e" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Best Linear Approximation of a Multivariable Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "7dd416d0-1ef2-51a8-93a4-3be0957eeb24" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "f(x,y,z)≈-3+2x-y+z" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186363057} {:answer/id #uuid "9433909f-0111-5198-840d-ad8d8b2b1180" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "f(x,y,z)≈2x+y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186363058} {:answer/id #uuid "3b318543-6d6b-5b8b-9bf4-82ed13590598" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "f(x,y,z)≈1-x+y-z" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186363059} {:answer/id #uuid "dd3de839-b10f-5875-85e0-f384a2825ab4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "f(x,y,z)≈-2+2y+3z" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186363060} {:answer/id #uuid "b36d70c2-de52-5d60-94b9-f3ac92184afb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "f(x,y,z)≈-x-y-2z" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186363061}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3b318543-6d6b-5b8b-9bf4-82ed13590598" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "f(x,y,z)≈1-x+y-z" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186363059} :answer-field/id #uuid "d2e319fa-8986-5bcf-b645-e666599902b9" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186363056}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "9fed137c-3970-57b9-8c2b-495f775fd2ec" :question/math-academy-id "q-129746" :question/problem "Let $f(x,y,z)=yz-x.$ Find the linear approximation of $f$ at the point $(0,-1,1).$" :question/worked-solution "The linear approximation of the function $f$ at the point $(a,b,c)$ is given by\n\n$$\nf(x,y,z)≈f(a,b,c)+∇f(a,b,c)\\cdot ⟨x-a,y-b,z-c⟩.\n$$\n\nSo, the linear approximation of $f$ at the point $(0,-1,1)$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,y,z) & ≈f(0,-1,1)+∇f(0,-1,1)\\cdot ⟨x,y+1,z-1⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the definition of $f,$ we have that $f(0,-1,1)=-1.$ Computing the partial derivatives, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{x} & =-1\\,⇒\\,{f}_{x}(0,-1,1)=-1, \\\\ {f}_{y} & =z\\,⇒\\,{f}_{y}(0,-1,1)=1, \\\\ {f}_{z} & =y\\,⇒\\,{f}_{z}(0,-1,1)=-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the gradient of the function at the point $(0,-1,1)$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(0,-1,1) & =⟨{f}_{x}(0,-1,1),{f}_{y}(0,-1,1),{f}_{z}(0,-1,1)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-1,1,-1⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the linear approximation of the function $f$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,y,z) & ≈f(0,-1,1)+∇f(0,-1,1)\\cdot ⟨x,y+1,z-1⟩ \\\\  & ≈-1+⟨-1,1,-1⟩\\cdot ⟨x,y+1,z-1⟩ \\\\  & ≈-1-(x)+(y+1)-(z-1) \\\\  & ≈1-x+y-z.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186363062 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "b1639105-dca4-50d1-8d86-a7aed03ba0b5" :knowledge-point/title "Estimating the Value of a Three-Variable Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "738ed73d-b486-521e-ad84-9e1de1647447" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3.15" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186363064} {:answer/id #uuid "cfa07642-7da2-524c-b6b8-94ba1ac508af" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3.03" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186363065} {:answer/id #uuid "24be30f9-d8d7-5da8-bf5a-75c0a7cd1df8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3.01" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186363066} {:answer/id #uuid "c2cc5401-76ab-5776-a2e9-af41f884cc7d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2.99" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186363067} {:answer/id #uuid "a9ae433e-248f-5100-a081-dfb1ef3fe8ac" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3.25" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186363068}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "738ed73d-b486-521e-ad84-9e1de1647447" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3.15" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186363064} :answer-field/id #uuid "0e5ed355-6a4f-5c4a-8d01-a99e4ac83ef1" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186363063}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "164023df-d37c-516f-9c3b-868f9fb8729b" :question/math-academy-id "q-98342" :question/problem "Suppose that $f(x,y,z)$ is a differentiable function such that\n\n$$\nf(1,2,3)=3,\\,{f}_{x}(1,2,3)=-2,\\,{f}_{y}(1,2,3)=1,\\,{f}_{z}(1,2,3)=3.\n$$\n\nUsing a linear approximation of $f$ at the point $(1,2,3)$ estimate the value of $f(0.96,1.98,3.03).$" :question/worked-solution "The linear approximation of the function $f$ at the point $(a,b,c)$ is given by\n\n$$\nf(x,y,z)≈f(a,b,c)+∇f(a,b,c)\\cdot ⟨x-a,y-b,z-c⟩.\n$$\n\nSo, the linear approximation of $f$ at the point $(1,2,3)$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,y,z) & ≈f(1,2,3)+∇f(1,2,3)\\cdot ⟨x-1,y-2,z-3⟩ \\\\  & =f(1,2,3)+⟨{f}_{x}(1,2,3),{f}_{y}(1,2,3),{f}_{z}(1,2,3)⟩\\cdot ⟨x-1,y-2,z-3⟩ \\\\  & =3+⟨-2,1,3⟩\\cdot ⟨x-1,y-2,z-3⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can approximate $f(0.96,1.98,3.03)$ by evaluating the linear approximation at $x=0.96,$ $y=1.98,$ and $z=3.03$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(0.96,1.98,3.03) & ≈3+⟨-2,1,3⟩\\cdot ⟨0.96-1,1.98-2,3.03-3⟩ \\\\  & =3+⟨-2,1,3⟩\\cdot ⟨-0.04,-0.02,0.03⟩ \\\\  & =3+0.08-0.02+0.09 \\\\  & =3.15\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186363069 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "b1639105-dca4-50d1-8d86-a7aed03ba0b5" :knowledge-point/title "Estimating the Value of a Three-Variable Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "cbc5f628-a45c-512b-bd99-96cf15997590" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "25.64" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186363071} {:answer/id #uuid "63814b85-18f2-54da-910c-a66a92fd2e57" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "25.06" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186363072} {:answer/id #uuid "1f19b13c-a104-52b1-96e2-d6ca0232a622" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "24.85" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186363073} {:answer/id #uuid "e9b75960-4d46-5822-aa76-d3169239ed59" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "24.78" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186363074} {:answer/id #uuid "48504aa5-c3c9-54fc-98d4-9105583a03b5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "24.99" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186363075}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "48504aa5-c3c9-54fc-98d4-9105583a03b5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "24.99" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186363075} :answer-field/id #uuid "5a967a5f-c3be-5a15-b50f-2bcf02c1822c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186363070}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "a6fb78aa-f2e7-500d-a880-3c8ff234bc2f" :question/math-academy-id "q-98347" :question/problem "Let $f(x,y,z)$ be a differentiable function such that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & f(3,0,4)=25, &  & {f}_{x}(x,y,z)=\\frac{5x}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}}}, \\\\  & {f}_{y}(x,y,z)=\\frac{5y}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}}}, &  & {f}_{z}(x,y,z)=\\frac{5z}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing a linear approximation of $f$ at the point $(3,0,4),$ estimate the value of $f(3.01,0.1,3.99).$" :question/worked-solution "The linear approximation of the function $f$ at the point $(a,b,c)$ is given by\n\n$$\nf(x,y,z)≈f(a,b,c)+∇f(a,b,c)\\cdot ⟨x-a,y-b,z-c⟩.\n$$\n\nSo, the linear approximation of $f$ at the point $(3,0,4)$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,y,z) & ≈f(3,0,4)+∇f(3,0,4)\\cdot ⟨x-3,y-0,z-4⟩ \\\\  & =25+⟨{f}_{x}(3,0,4),{f}_{y}(3,0,4),{f}_{z}(3,0,4)⟩\\cdot ⟨x-3,y,z-4⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we find ${f}_{x}(3,0,4)$, ${f}_{y}(3,0,4)$ and ${f}_{z}(3,0,4),$ using the given partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{x}(3,0,4) & =\\frac{5(3)}{\\sqrt{{3}^{2}+{0}^{2}+{4}^{2}}}=3 \\\\ {f}_{y}(3,0,4) & =\\frac{5(0)}{\\sqrt{{3}^{2}+{0}^{2}+{4}^{2}}}=0 \\\\ {f}_{z}(3,0,4) & =\\frac{5(4)}{\\sqrt{{3}^{2}+{0}^{2}+{4}^{2}}}=4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\nf(x,y,z)≈25+⟨3,0,4⟩\\cdot ⟨x-3,y,z-4⟩.\n$$\n\nFinally, we can estimate $f(3.01,0.1,3.99)$ by evaluating the linear approximation at $x=3.01,y=0.1$ and $z=3.99,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(3.01,0.1,3.99) & ≈25+⟨3,0,4⟩\\cdot ⟨3.01-3,0.1,3.99-4⟩ \\\\  & =25+⟨3,0,4⟩\\cdot ⟨0.01,0.1,-0.01⟩ \\\\  & =25+0.03-0.04 \\\\  & =24.99\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186335381 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "0015e19e-222b-5c5b-a020-93392c50c10e" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Best Linear Approximation of a Multivariable Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b2a6f096-41d9-5250-bd7e-b95d0b6e4a05" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "f(x,y)≈2\\operatorname{ln}⁡2-x-y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186335383} {:answer/id #uuid "9c3db9ab-4bdc-52c1-bd78-8d2f25ce180e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "f(x,y)≈2\\operatorname{ln}⁡2+2x+y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186335384} {:answer/id #uuid "00243150-770f-5f4f-8449-bfc02dcdfce0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "f(x,y)≈-2-x+y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186335385} {:answer/id #uuid "feb991f1-4050-58ab-86f0-3cb5c2eb15c0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "f(x,y)≈-2+2x-2y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186335386} {:answer/id #uuid "daca5d82-050b-5f20-8c5f-4bab365f7108" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "f(x,y)≈2\\operatorname{ln}⁡2+2x-2y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186335387}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "daca5d82-050b-5f20-8c5f-4bab365f7108" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "f(x,y)≈2\\operatorname{ln}⁡2+2x-2y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186335387} :answer-field/id #uuid "76acc3b3-2256-500e-853d-ac36a9df1535" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186335382}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "02cd9397-a795-5b68-a5d4-f55d0d92f60a" :question/math-academy-id "q-98553" :question/problem "Let $f(x,y)=x{e}^{y}.$ Find the linear approximation of $f$ at the point $(-1,\\operatorname{ln}⁡2).$" :question/worked-solution "The linear approximation of the function $f$ at the point $(a,b)$ is given by\n\n$$\nf(x,y)≈f(a,b)+∇f(a,b)\\cdot ⟨x-a,y-b⟩.\n$$\n\nSo, the linear approximation of $f$ at the point $(-1,\\operatorname{ln}⁡2)$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,y) & ≈f(-1,\\operatorname{ln}⁡2)+∇f(-1,\\operatorname{ln}⁡2)\\cdot ⟨x+1,y-\\operatorname{ln}⁡2⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the definition of $f,$ we have that $f(-1,\\operatorname{ln}⁡2)=-2.$ Computing the partial derivatives, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{x} & ={e}^{y}\\, &  & ⇒\\, & {f}_{x}(-1,\\operatorname{ln}⁡2)={e}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}=2, \\\\ {f}_{y} & =x{e}^{y} &  & ⇒\\, & {f}_{y}(-1,\\operatorname{ln}⁡2)=-1{e}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}=-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the gradient of the function at the point $(-1,\\operatorname{ln}⁡2)$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(-1,\\operatorname{ln}⁡2) & =⟨2,-2⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the linear approximation of the function $f$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,y) & ≈f(-1,\\operatorname{ln}⁡2)+∇\\,f(-1,\\operatorname{ln}⁡2)\\cdot ⟨x+1,\\,y-\\operatorname{ln}⁡2⟩ \\\\  & =-2+⟨2,-2⟩\\cdot ⟨x+1,\\,y-\\operatorname{ln}⁡2⟩ \\\\  & =-2+2(x+1)-2(y-\\operatorname{ln}⁡2) \\\\  & =2\\operatorname{ln}⁡2+2x-2y.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}]}
